      "segmentId": "m00028:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00028:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "In a reaction, Lewis base B, with a filled orbital, and Lewis acid A, with a vacant orbital, form the product molecule BA."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_02_11_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00001",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00028:title:sect-00001-title",
        "text": "Lewis Acids and the Curved Arrow Formalism"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00002",
          "segmentId": "m00028:para:para-00002"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00003",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00028:alt:fig-00003-alt",
              "text": "A reaction shows Lewis acid (M g 2 positive) reacting with Lewis base (an organodiphosphate ion) to form an acid-base complex."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_02_11_002.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00003",
          "segmentId": "m00028:para:para-00003"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00001",
          "class": "scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00028:caption:fig-00001-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00028:alt:fig-00001-alt",
              "text": "A reversible reaction shows boron trifluoride (Lewis acid) reacting with dimethyl ether (Lewis base) to form an acid-base complex. Electrostatic potential maps of reactants and product are shown above."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_02_11_003.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00004",
          "segmentId": "m00028:para:para-00004"
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00005",
          "segmentId": "m00028:para:para-00005"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00004",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00028:alt:fig-00004-alt",
              "text": "Some neutral proton donors, such as water, carboxylic acid, phenol, as well as some cations like lithium ions, and some metal compounds like aluminum chloride, are collectively labeled Lewis acids."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_02_11_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00002",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00028:title:sect-00002-title",
        "text": "Lewis Bases"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00006",
          "segmentId": "m00028:para:para-00006"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00005",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00028:alt:fig-00005-alt",
              "text": "In a reversible reaction, hydrogen chloride acting as an acid reacts with water acting as a base, to form a hydronium ion and chloride ion."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_02_11_005.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00007",
          "segmentId": "m00028:para:para-00007"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00006",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00028:alt:fig-00006-alt",
              "text": "Some Lewis bases are alcohol, ether, aldehyde, ketone, acid chloride, carboxylic acid, ester, amide, amine, sulfide, and organotriphosphate ion."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_02_11_006.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00008",
          "segmentId": "m00028:para:para-00008"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00007",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00028:alt:fig-00007-alt",
              "text": "In a reversible reaction, acetic acid (base) reacts with sulfuric acid to form two resonance structures of a protonated product. The not formed product shows protonation on single bonded oxygen."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_02_11_007.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
